{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1451544-beats-clues-doors-and-tilts","id":1451544,"title":"Beats, Clues, Doors and Tilts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-10T09:02:52-07:00","id":1451544,"title":"Beats, Clues, Doors and Tilts","content":"So I started with NWoD about 14 years ago. Haven't run a ton of games, played in even less, but still. Got comfortable with the system.<br /> <br /> Now I'm trying to organize a game of ChroD, and I like what they've changed.<br /> But the thing is...a lot of my players are just. Really, really bad at anything resembling mechanics.<br /> <br /> To offer a brief glimpse: I introduced a couple of them to ttrpg's through the d20 system in a game that ran for 10 3-hour sessions. I still had several of them asking me &quot;wait, which one's the d8?&quot; after almost 40 hours of play.<br /> Another player who, after playing 3rd D&amp;D for about 20 years, still asks me &quot;what does Charging do again?&quot; multiple times in one session.<br /> <br /> So with that said, the sub systems for things like Beats, Clues, Doors and Tilts are all really interesting...but I don't feel comfortable enough with them to run a game where literally every piece of the game's mechanics must be calculated, processed and conveyed by me and me alone.<br /> <br /> Does anyone have a super rules-lite version of these subsystems, or some useful shorthand for them? If I could fit the jist of them onto one index card each, I think I could manage the rest.<br /> <br /> It's...just indescribably exhausting sometimes.<br /> I mean, don't get me wrong. I love the story. The story is everything. But...the crunch is fun, too. It let's you flex that other part of your brain and gives you the kind of instant gratification that is normally reserved for video games. I just wish these people would take the initiative for their own understanding. But I know that, in some cases at least, they're literally doing the best they can.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T09:51:01-07:00","id":1451546,"title":"","content":"From memory, as I'm away from my books at the moment:<br /> <br /> Beats are literally just experience points broken down into a more digestible number than 1e's range of costs that ran from 2 to 80 -- Experiences are earned from Beats at a one-per-five rate and traded in for character advancement. <br /> <br /> Doors are the number of successful rolls you need in Social Maneuvering (which can use non-Social traits) to convince someone to do something for you.<br /> <br /> Clues are sources of dice modifiers in the course of semi-procedurally generating the results of an Investigation. You get more and better Clues by using different Skills and leaning into your Specialties.<br /> <br /> Tilts are mostly just environmental hazards and personal injury effects. They're relevant in action scenes like fights and chases.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T09:59:02-07:00","id":1451547,"title":"","content":"You can safely just ignore Clues and Doors. The Investigation system works best for a type of cooperative mystery story which seems to me to be a rare approach to this kind of game. Social Manoeuvring is a lot more useful since it can fit into many (but far from all) types of social interactions common to games. But in either case, they're fully optional and you don't have to engage with them. Depending on your style of GMing and the planned story, they might not even be relevant (especially the Investigation system).<br /> <br /> Tilts and Conditions are designed to fit onto index cards. It's gonna be a lot of cards if you want to prepare them beforehand, but an option is to just write it down on index cards or something similar as they become relevant during play.<br /> <br /> Beats are just one fifth of an XP. There's no real system behind them, unless you mean how to earn them. There's generally a list in each core book detailing how a player earn beats and the whole list fits easily on an index card.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T11:23:07-07:00","id":1451556,"title":"","content":"Sorry, I should clarify. I didn't mean I literally needed help with, say, *doors*, specifically. Just that Social Manuevering in general seems like a pretty involved subsystem. Same with the investigation rules.<br /> And while I love the simplified way you spend experience, the way you gain it is much more complex. Which is actually even worse from a design position; you only spend xp after you have enough of it saved up. So a complicated xp cost system is something you deal with pretty rarely. But the stuff with dramatic failures and resolving conditions--those are things every player needs to be thinking about all the while they play the game. So if you have players who can barely hold one mathematical process in their head, adding another will be arduous.<br /> <br /> I think I'll ignore the stuff on investigations and social manuevering. I like the idea behind them, but I have enough experience under my belt to make those sorts of scenes run smoothly without getting bogged down in rules or ignoring them all together--the two extremes that both lead to dissatisfying stories.<br /> <br /> Beats...hm. I'll probably just water down that whole concept a bit for these players.<br /> <br /> I assume there's a big ol' Deck of Tilts somewhere I could get ahold of?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T13:02:49-07:00","id":1451566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1451544-beats-clues-doors-and-tilts?p=1451556#post1451556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And while I love the simplified way you spend experience, the way you gain it is much more complex. Which is actually even worse from a design position; you only spend xp after you have enough of it saved up. So a complicated xp cost system is something you deal with pretty rarely. But the stuff with dramatic failures and resolving conditions--those are things every player needs to be thinking about all the while they play the game. So if you have players who can barely hold one mathematical process in their head, adding another will be arduous.</div> </div> </div> </div>Call every beat for an XP, multiply all XP costs by five, and write out the already compiled list of beat gains on one index card per player. On the back you could write the adjusted XP costs for traits, just to avoid any confusion. Now the players have literally zero math to do (that isn't inherent to absolutely all point-buy systems) and literally all relevant info in the palm of their hand.<br /> If your players can't handle that despite all the work you've put down for them (writing out all this stuff) then you seriously need to start putting some responsibilities on the players. Tell them it's up to them to remember how they earn XP because it comes from their own actions and is not handed out by you. If they don't remember that (despite how ridiculously easy it is) then it's their loss.<br /> <br /> There's Condition and Tilt cards on drivethrurpg, though I'd personally just copy-paste from the book (if you have it as pdf and not just hardcopy) into a word processor of your choice and print it yourself rather than spend the money.<br /> <br /> Edit: I empathise with players who don't remember every single detail of a game, but they seriously need to take some responsibility in learning how to play and not be held by the hand by the player who already has by far the highest work load (aka the GM).<br /> When I start playing in a new game, I try to write down what I need to remember that's relevant for my character even if it's not something normally noted on a character sheet. For example, I recently started playing a game of Black Crusade. When I saw the list of combat actions available and realised I wouldn't be able to remember it all, I sat down and wrote down every action relevant to my character with single line descriptions I could check up at a moment's notice without having to ask &quot;what can my character do?&quot; or bring out the book every time.<br /> And when I'm planning my actions and realise I don't quite remember how something works (like, say, a charge action) I try to look it up before it's my turn in the game so I hold up the game as little as possible. (Obviously this depends on your ability to pay attention at the same time. Losing track of what's going on and having to be brought up to speed is also disruptive.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 06-10-2021, 04:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T15:22:03-07:00","id":1451586,"title":"","content":"I get the confusion / being overwhelmed with Beats - and Conditions specifically. I\u2019m playing in a CoD game right now, and even being familiar with the rules and specifically seeking out opportunities to gain / resolve conditions, it\u2019s tricky and it takes skill. There\u2019s so many different ways a condition can be earned (even though it\u2019s usually just exceptional successes), and when you can earn one, it\u2019s sometimes hard to find one that seems to fit the situation. Plus the other main source of Beats, Aspirations, can take practice to right good ones for. They\u2019re not necessarily complex systems to grok, but they do take an active, creative player and some experience to use effectively.<br /> <br /> A way that you can help as a Storyteller is finding a handful of conditions that will be your \u201cgo to\u201d - easy to resolve, easy to customize - and hand those out as often as possible. Informed, Inspired , and Steadfast make good \u2018positive\u2019 conditions to reward an exceptional success. Spooked and Disoriented are good penalties when something puts them off balance. The fluff for these can generally be tailored to any circumstance, so as a Storyteller you can hand them as often as possible. Plus they all have a straightforward mechanical effect - do it, get a Beat - which makes notecards for them easy to pass out.<br /> <br /> Another thing STs can do is offer dramatic failures. If any action resolution occurs and you think \u201cI can make this situation worse\u201d, offer them a Beat.<br /> <br /> Finally, you can present very strong signals for what will happen during session: this is important for Aspirations. Tell your players to leave one or two aspirations blank every session, present the scenarios you want to introduce early and clearly, and have them jot down what they want to have happen to their character. And it\u2019s okay if it\u2019s just \u201cdo the plot hook\u201d. As they get more familiarity with their characters and the system, encourage them to tailor \u201cdo the plot hook\u201d to the character\u2019s talents and bias. And develop long term goals, and the like. But the part the Storyteller can influence is being clear about what scenes will happen, so that players can write short term aspirations that will actually come up.<br /> <br /> Hope that helps,<br /> ~ Seraph Kitty","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}